# # 2. Per-shape sub-distribution

Each sub-shape is computed within the primary set from §1 (e.g. "Float" sub-shapes are computed over the 592 Float-using fixtures). Sub-shapes can overlap (a fixture using `let n: Float = ...` also uses `Float` literals).

# ## 2.1 Float (primary: 592)

| Sub-shape | Count | pass | no-op | REFUSED | Success |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `let [mut] n: Float = <expr>` (const-fold chain) | 561 | 0 | 135 | 426 | 0.0% |
| Float arithmetic `+ - * /` (2+ Float tokens) | 24 | 1 | 18 | 5 | 4.2% |
| Float in `fn` signature | 11 | 1 | 9 | 1 | 9.1% |
| `Float` used as a type only (no const-fold) | 561 | 0 | 135 | 426 | 0.0% |

The const-fold chain (561/592 = 94.8% of Float fixtures) is the single sub-shape that drives the 156 silent Float miscompiles. The non-const-fold Float usages (31 fixtures) have a different distribution but are still mostly silent.

# ## 2.2 3-arm match (primary: 16)

| Sub-shape | Count | pass | no-op | REFUSED | Success |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3-arm on plain Int | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 100.0% |
| 3-arm with `Some/None` | 4 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 50.0% |
| 3-arm with `Ok/Err` (Result) | 2 | 0 | 2 | 0 | 0.0% |
| 4+ arm match (any) | 15 | 5 | 6 | 4 | 33.3% |

4+ arm match dominates (15/16 = 93.8%). The 3-arm Ok/Err variant (2 fixtures, both silent) is the niche where Result + 3-arm meet.

# ## 2.3 Result[T] (primary: 241)

| Sub-shape | Count | pass | no-op | REFUSED | Success |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `-> Result[T]` return type | 42 | 14 | 17 | 11 | 33.3% |
| `Ok(...)` literal | 121 | 17 | 81 | 23 | 14.0% |
| `Err(...)` literal | 114 | 16 | 80 | 18 | 14.0% |
| Result in match pattern | 46 | 9 | 27 | 10 | 19.6% |
| Result chained (`?`, `and_then`, `or_else`) | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | n/a |

`Ok(...)` and `Err(...)` literals account for 161 of the 179 Result no-ops (90.0%). The chained variant (`?`, `and_then`) does not appear in any fixture in the v2 set — this sub-shape is residual work, not a current gap.

# ## 2.4 struct (primary: 75, all match the `struct` keyword)

| Sub-shape | Count | pass | no-op | REFUSED | Success |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `type X struct { ... }` def | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | n/a |
| struct literal `Foo { x: 1 }` | 72 | 4 | 44 | 24 | 5.6% |
| field access `.x` | 69 | 4 | 42 | 23 | 5.8% |
| struct in match pattern | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0.0% |
| struct as fn arg | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | n/a |

The struct and field-access sub-shapes track each other (72/69 ≈ 1.0 ratio — almost every struct-using fixture also has field access). v2's "field/type" detector catches more fixtures (277) by including fixtures that use `Type` and `.x` without the `struct` keyword; this report's struct sub-shapes are restricted to the explicit-keyword set, which is the same as `x86_emit_struct`'s matcher shape (x86-shadow-features.oot §1 row 55).

# ## 2.5 List[T] (primary: 264)

| Sub-shape | Count | pass | no-op | REFUSED | Success |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `List[T]` type | 264 | 40 | 162 | 62 | 15.2% |
| `list_new(...)` | 209 | 11 | 143 | 55 | 5.3% |
| `list_push(...)` | 192 | 7 | 136 | 49 | 3.6% |
| `list_get(...)` | 107 | 6 | 77 | 24 | 5.6% |
| `list_len(...)` | 90 | 0 | 66 | 24 | 0.0% |

`List[T]` type is the umbrella; every List-using fixture has it. `list_len` is the worst single sub-shape at 0% pass. `list_new` and `list_push` are the highest-count silent-miscompile sub-shapes (143 + 136 = 279 silent miscompiles, more than half the total no-op count for List).

